[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering GraphQL: Types, Scalars, and Interfaces"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"GraphQL relies heavily on a strongly typed schema to define the capabilities of a service. Understanding how types, scalars, and interfaces work together is fundamental to designing robust and scalable APIs."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding GraphQL Scalars"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Scalar types represent the leaf nodes of a GraphQL query. They resolve to concrete data primitives. GraphQL comes with a set of default scalar types out of the box."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Int: A signed 32-bit integer."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Float: A signed double-precision floating-point value."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"String: A UTF-8 character sequence."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Boolean: True or false."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ID: A unique identifier, often used to refetch an object or as a cache key. Serialized like a String."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Custom Scalars"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define custom scalars when the default primitives are not enough, such as for dates or URLs. Below is an example of defining a custom Date scalar in your schema."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"scalar Date

type Event {
  id: ID!
  title: String!
  startDate: Date!
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Object Types and Fields"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object types are the most common components of a GraphQL schema. They represent a pool of data you can fetch, containing a set of fields that resolve to either scalars or other object types."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"type User {
  id: ID!
  username: String!
  email: String
  friends: [User!]!
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Working with Interfaces"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"An interface is an abstract type that includes a certain set of fields that a type must include to implement the interface. Interfaces are extremely useful when you want to return an object or a list of objects that might be of different types, but share common fields."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"interface Character {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  friends: [Character]
}

type Human implements Character {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  friends: [Character]
  totalCredits: Float
}

type Droid implements Character {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  friends: [Character]
  primaryFunction: String
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Querying Interfaces"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"When querying a field that returns an interface, you often need to use inline fragments (using the ... on syntax) to access fields specific to the underlying implementing type."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"query {
  hero {
    id
    name
    ... on Human {
      totalCredits
    }
    ... on Droid {
      primaryFunction
    }
  }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"By effectively combining custom scalars, strict types, and flexible interfaces, you can build a powerful, self-documenting GraphQL API contract that scales cleanly."}]
